course: medicine
lesson: 33
title: Being in hospital, and a sick parent or grandparent
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When you or someone you love is seriously ill, it is normal to feel scared, sad, angry, jealous or all of them at once, and none of these feelings makes you a bad person.
explain: Illness is never a child's fault: not a quarrel, not a bad thought, not something you forgot to do caused it, and you cannot catch cancer or depression from someone.
explain: Hospitals look frightening with their beeping machines, but the beeps are mostly reminders for nurses, and a children's ward lets a parent stay with you.
explain: Picture Sam, whose mum is in hospital: he helps at home, worries at night and feels guilty when he laughs with friends; the best thing he can do is ask his questions and tell a grown-up he trusts how he feels.
fact: Children's wards let a parent stay and have play areas and friendly staff, so a child in hospital is not left alone.
fact: Children in hospital can often keep in touch with friends through visits, video calls, cards and messages.
fact: It is natural for a child to feel scared, sad or angry when a parent is ill, and the illness is never the child's fault.
fact: Cancer is when some cells grow out of control; there are many types, many people get better with treatment, and you cannot catch it from someone.
fact: Chemotherapy kills fast-growing cells, including cancer cells and hair cells, so hair often falls out and usually grows back after treatment.
fact: Depression is an illness that affects mood, energy and thinking; it is not laziness or just being sad, and doctors treat it with talking therapy and sometimes medicine.
fact: When someone is dying, doctors and nurses, including hospice and palliative care teams, keep them comfortable and free of pain, and support the family.
fact: People near the end of life may still hear voices, so it is fine to talk to them, and it is fine to cry.
fact: Children who help look after an ill or disabled family member are called young carers, and schools and councils can support them.
fact: Feeling jealous when a sick brother or sister gets lots of attention is normal and doesn't make you a bad person.
fact: Hospital machines monitor heart rate, oxygen and breathing and beep when something needs checking, which is often not urgent.
fact: Most illnesses aren't passed from parent to child, and doctors can answer questions about family risk.
exercise: Will you be left alone on a children's ward? | No, a parent can usually stay with you | Yes, parents must go home when visiting ends | Yes, after 8pm, when the lights go out | Only if you're under 2
exercise: How can a child in hospital keep in touch with friends? | Visits, video calls, cards and messages | They can't at all, phones aren't allowed | Only by letter after they leave | Only by shouting to them from the window
exercise: Is it your fault if your parent gets ill? | No, illness is never the child's fault | Yes, if you were naughty or rude to them | Yes, if you argued | Only if you forgot their birthday that year
exercise: What is cancer? | Cells growing out of control | A germ you catch from other people's coughs | A broken bone deep inside the body | A kind of very bad mood that lasts
exercise: Can you catch cancer from Grandma? | No | Yes, by hugging her too close | Yes, by sharing a cup of tea | Only if you live with her
exercise: Why does chemotherapy make hair fall out? | It kills fast-growing cells, including hair cells | It's a poison that ruins every bit of your hair forever | The cancer eats hair | The hospital shampoo is far too strong
exercise: Does hair usually grow back after chemotherapy? | Yes, usually after treatment ends | No, never, it stays bald for good | Only on the heads of children, not adults | Only if you eat carrots
exercise: What is depression? | An illness affecting mood, energy and thinking | Just being lazy and not wanting to try | Feeling a bit sad for one afternoon after school | Something you catch from somebody else's sneeze, like a cold
exercise: How do doctors treat depression? | Talking therapy and sometimes medicine | By telling people to cheer up and smile more | With strong antibiotics for the infection | With a long stay in bed and lots of sleep
exercise: What do hospice and palliative care teams do? | Keep dying people comfortable and support families | Perform big operations to save their lives | Give vaccines to babies | Run hospital schools
exercise: Is it OK to talk to someone who is dying? | Yes, they may still hear you | No, it disturbs them and makes it worse | No, it's not allowed | Only doctors may talk
exercise: What is a young carer? | A child who helps look after an ill or disabled family member | A nurse who is still under 18 | A babysitter paid by the hour | A child who is ill
exercise: Is feeling jealous of a sick brother wrong? | No, it's normal | Yes, it makes you a bad brother or sister | Yes, you must hide it for ever | Only if you say it aloud
exercise: Why do hospital machines beep? | Something needs checking, often not urgent | The patient is always in terrible danger when they beep | They are broken | They are playing a tune
exercise: Will you definitely get the same illness as your parent? | No, most illnesses aren't passed on | Yes, always, because it runs in families | Yes, at the same age | Only boys do
test: "grandma's bald so the chemo isn't working" — right? | No, hair loss doesn't mean it's failing | Yes, bald means it has failed and stopped | Yes, hair shows how well it works | No, she was always bald
test: Dad's in bed all day with depression. Your cousin says "he's just lazy". Truth? | Depression is an illness, not laziness | Your cousin is right, he should get up | It's because of you | He'll be fine if you shout to wake him up
test: You argued with Mum and next day she went into hospital. Did you cause it? | No, an argument can't cause illness | Yes, it was your fault for upsetting her | Maybe, if you shouted really loudly at her | Yes, worry makes others ill
test: Machines round Grandad beep constantly. What should you do if worried? | Ask a nurse what the machines do | Unplug them so he can rest | Run out of the hospital | Shout for a doctor every single time one beeps
test: Grandad is dying and you don't know what to say. What helps? | Say what you want, hold his hand, and it's fine to cry | Say nothing, he can't hear anything now | Stay away so you don't upset him | Pretend nothing's happening
test: You look after your mum, do shopping and help her wash. Who could help you? | Your teacher and the school can support young carers | Nobody, you must cope with it all by yourself | Only the police | Only your friends' parents
test: "Brother gets all the attention since he got ill, I hate it" — what's the best step? | Tell your parents how you feel | Keep it secret forever | Pretend to be ill too, to get some attention | Break his toys
test: What would happen if a scared child in hospital had nobody to explain things? | She'd likely feel more frightened and alone | She'd heal faster and more quietly without all the chatter | Nothing changes, children don't need explaining to | She'd feel braver
test: In a soap opera a kid catches cancer from his best mate. What's wrong? | Cancer can't be caught from someone | Nothing, it spreads like flu does | Only in hot countries, where it spreads more | Only between best friends who share everything
test: Mum has an illness and you fear you'll get it too. What's sensible? | Ask your doctor or parent, most illnesses aren't passed on | Assume you'll get it one day too | Never talk about it | Stop eating
test: Max is in hospital and his class forgets to write. What could they do? | Send cards or video messages | Wait until he's back and say hello then | Nothing, hospitals don't allow post | Visit with a cold
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the transmission of cancer between persons. | Cancer is not transmitted from one person to another | Cancer is transmitted by close physical contact | Cancer is transmitted through coughing or sneezing | Cancer is transmitted by the sharing of cups and cutlery
exam: Identify the principal reason for which chemotherapy frequently occasions the loss of hair. | It destroys rapidly dividing cells, including those of the hair | It is administered by injection through the scalp | It indicates the failure of the treatment | The disease itself consumes the roots of the hair
exam: Ascertain the most accurate characterisation of depression. | An illness affecting mood, energy and thought | A form of idleness and lack of will | A transient sadness of an afternoon | An infection transmitted by contact
exam: Determine the designation customarily given to a child who assists in the care of an ill or disabled member of the family. | A young carer | A junior nurse | A ward assistant | A palliative specialist
